*** IMPORTANT ***

Rule A — Starting a transfer

When the Arm CPU writes 1 to CONTROL bit 0, your AXI module generates a one-clock-cycle start pulse. A transfer is only accepted if the SPI master is idle and the receive FIFO has space.

If SPI is busy or the FIFO is full, the command is rejected and a sticky error flag is set.

Rule B — Receiving data

A completed SPI transfer writes one 16-bit word into the RX FIFO. Received values are stored in the order they arrive. No previous unread data should be overwritten.

Rule C — Reading an empty FIFO

An AXI read of RX_DATA while empty returns zero with the AXI error response SLVERR.

Rule D — Invalid addresses

Unmapped register accesses and writes to read-only registers return SLVERR. They must not change internal state.

Rule E — AXI backpressure

An AXI response remains valid, with stable data and response status, until the processor accepts it. The slave can handle one outstanding read and one outstanding write in this initial implementation.

Rule F — Reset and error clearing

Reset clears the control state, FIFO pointers/count, and sticky flags. CONTROL bit 1 requests a FIFO flush, and bit 2 clears sticky done/error flags.

Rule G — Partial writes

The AXI WSTRB byte enables control which bytes of TX_DATA and CLK_DIV are updated. CONTROL commands execute only when byte lane 0 is enabled. A write with all strobes zero has no effect.

Rule H — FIFO read timing

Your current FIFO uses synchronous registered reads. We'll have the AXI interface retrieve a word into a response holding register before asserting RVALID.

The FIFO's internal read pointer may advance while preparing that response, but the word is retained in the AXI response register until the processor accepts it. This provides correct bus-visible behavior during stalls.

There is one additional detail for your later integration: simultaneous software commands, SPI completion, and reset/flush events need a defined priority. I recommend reset first, then flush, then normal transaction handling.